    <div class="container-narrow">
      <p style="font-size:0.8125rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-bottom:1rem;"><a href="/" style="color:var(--color-accent);">Home</a> &rsaquo; Women's Hanfu</p>
      <h1>Women's Hanfu: Flowing Silhouettes for Every Body and Occasion</h1>
      <p class="lead" style="font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-top:1.5rem;line-height:1.55;">
        Women's Hanfu is the heart of the Hanfu revival. From the gauzy romance of Tang ruqun to the structured elegance of Ming aoqun, women's Hanfu spans an extraordinary range of silhouettes, colors, and moods. There is a style and a fit for every body type, every budget, and every occasion.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0;">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <div class="media-card" style="margin-bottom:2.5rem; grid-template-columns: 38% 62%;">
        <div class="media-card-img">
          <img src="/images/pexels-hanfu-nanjing.jpg" srcset="/images/opt/pexels-hanfu-nanjing-400w.webp 400w, /images/opt/pexels-hanfu-nanjing-600w.webp 600w" sizes="(max-width: 768px) 100vw, 420px" alt="People in traditional Chinese hanfu strolling through a city street" width="1400" height="933" loading="lazy">
        </div>
        <div class="media-card-body" style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:center;">
          <p style="font-size:0.8125rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:0.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--color-accent);margin-bottom:0.5rem;">The Look</p>
          <h3 style="font-size:1.375rem;">One silhouette, endless dynastic dials.</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.9375rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-top:0.75rem;">Women's Hanfu spans three thousand years of looks. The right dynasty choice comes down to your body type, the occasion, and the mood you want to project.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Choosing the Right Dynasty Style for Your Body Type</h2>
      <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
        <table class="size-ref-table" style="margin-top:1rem;">
          <thead><tr><th>Body Type</th><th>Best Dynasty Style</th><th>Why It Works</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Petite</strong></td><td>Song Dynasty, Modern Hanfu</td><td>Slim, vertical silhouettes do not overwhelm a smaller frame. Beizi jackets elongate.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Tall</strong></td><td>Tang Dynasty, Ming Dynasty</td><td>Tang's high waist and Ming's floor-length skirts leverage height dramatically.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Curvy / Hourglass</strong></td><td>Tang Dynasty, Ming Dynasty</td><td>Tang high waist highlights the bust. Ming mamianqun's A-line defines the waist.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Athletic / Straight</strong></td><td>Ming Dynasty, Modern Hanfu</td><td>Ming's structured shape creates curves. Modern Hanfu's relaxed fit is naturally comfortable.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Plus Size</strong></td><td>Ming Dynasty, Tang Dynasty</td><td>Adjustable waist ties accommodate fluctuating sizes. Loose, flowing cuts are naturally forgiving.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2rem;">Women's Hanfu by Occasion</h2>
      <div class="grid-2" style="max-width:100%;margin-top:1rem;">
        <div class="vs-card">
          <h3>Daily Casual</h3>
          <p>Modern Hanfu or simple Ming mamianqun in cotton or linen. Easy to put on, comfortable for all-day wear, machine-washable. Pair with minimalist flats and a single hairpin.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="vs-card">
          <h3>Festival & Social</h3>
          <p>Tang ruqun in a single bold color or Song beizi set in pastels. These styles photograph beautifully and feel festive without being over-the-top formal.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="vs-card">
          <h3>Photoshoot</h3>
          <p>Tang Dynasty with daxiu shan outer robe -- the wide sleeves, translucent layers, and long train create the most dramatic photos. Choose complementary colors for depth.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="vs-card">
          <h3>Wedding & Formal</h3>
          <p>Tang bridal ruqun (dramatic) or Ming bridal aoqun (structured). Red silk with gold phoenix embroidery. Full accessory set including elaborate headdress.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2rem;">Women's Hanfu Price Ranges</h2>
      <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
        <table class="size-ref-table" style="margin-top:1rem;">
          <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Price (USD)</th><th>What to Expect</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Starter</strong></td><td>$40 -- $80</td><td>Basic cotton or polyester set, simple cut, few or no embroidery details</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Everyday Quality</strong></td><td>$80 -- $180</td><td>Cotton/linen/silk-blend, well-constructed, subtle embroidery, good fit</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Formal & Special Occasion</strong></td><td>$180 -- $500</td><td>Full silk or high-quality blends, intricate embroidery, layered sets with outer robes</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Bridal & Luxury</strong></td><td>$500 -- $2,500+</td><td>Custom-made, hand-embroidered, premium silk, artisan craftsmanship</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2rem;">Recommended Sellers</h2>
      <ul style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.5rem;color:var(--color-muted);font-size:0.9375rem;line-height:1.8;">
        <li><strong>Hanfu Story:</strong> Best overall for women's Hanfu -- reliable quality, consistent sizing, international shipping.</li>
        <li><strong>NewMoonDance:</strong> Excellent Song and Ming options with good historical accuracy.</li>
        <li><strong>Nuwa Hanfu:</strong> Premium custom women's Hanfu, especially for formal and bridal occasions.</li>
        <li><strong>Taobao -- Shisanyu:</strong> One of China's most popular women's Hanfu brands, known for beautiful Tang-style designs.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">How to Experience Women’s Hanfu Culture in China</h2>
      <p class="text-muted">Women’s Hanfu has become a social movement. Across China, women gather for Hanfu meetups, tea ceremonies, and cultural festivals. These cities offer the richest experiences.</p>
      <div class="vs-card" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">
        <h3>Chengdu – Hanfu Social Scene</h3>
        <p><strong>Why here:</strong> Chengdu has China’s most active women’s Hanfu community. Weekend Hanfu meetups at People’s Park, tea-house gatherings, and Hanfu-themed cafe afternoons are regular social life. The Chengdu Hanfu Association organizes monthly events open to visitors. “Hanfu Street” near Chunxi Road has a dozen women’s Hanfu shops.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Transportation:</strong> Fly into Chengdu Shuangliu. Metro Line 2/3 to Chunxi Road. Taxis from 8 RMB.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Where to stay:</strong> Chunxi Road / Taikoo Li (400-1,200 RMB/night). Kuanzhai Xiangzi boutique hotels (350-700 RMB/night). The Temple House (1,500-3,000 RMB/night) for luxury.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Local food:</strong> Beyond hot pot: danhong gao (5-10 RMB), tianshui mian (10-15 RMB). Wangjianglou Park tea houses are popular with Hanfu patrons.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Hanfu activities:</strong> Monthly “Hanfu Day” at People’s Park draws hundreds. Join a Hanfu tea ceremony under the bamboo groves at Wangjianglou Park.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="vs-card" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">
        <h3>Hangzhou – Hanfu by the Lake</h3>
        <p><strong>Why here:</strong> West Lake is China’s most popular women’s Hanfu destination. On any weekend, you will see dozens of women in Hanfu on the causeways and arched bridges. The aesthetic is overwhelmingly Song Dynasty. Hanfu rental shops provide complete Song-style looks for 150-300 RMB/day.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Transportation:</strong> Fly into Hangzhou Xiaoshan or train from Shanghai (1 hour). Metro Line 1 to Longxiangqiao. Lake best explored on foot or rental bicycle (1-2 RMB/30 min).</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Where to stay:</strong> West Lake scenic hotels (500-2,000 RMB/night). Longxiangqiao / Hubin area (400-900 RMB/night). Tea plantation guesthouse in Longjing Village (400-800 RMB/night) for a unique stay.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Local food:</strong> Louwailou on the lake shore (100-200 RMB/person) since 1848. Lakeside tea houses serve light meals (50-80 RMB/person).</p>
        <p style="margin-top:0.75rem;"><strong>Hanfu activities:</strong> Rent Hanfu at Hefang Street, spend the day around West Lake (12 km circuit). Su Causeway at dawn is the best photo spot. Mid-April to mid-May is the best season.</p>
      </div>
